    "thinking": "We are asked to integrate transformations from three sources: 42835f811f6644e1 (A), 16610bf6dded432d (B), c4ed9b25255b4c9f (C). We need to produce a new solution that combines the best aspects. The goal is to achieve high performance on ARM64 with OpenMP, using fast input, integral image, blocking, NEON SIMD, and parallelization.\n\nFrom previous experiments:\n- Source A alone gave mean_seconds 0.01538 (very fast) but without parallelism? Actually source A had no OpenMP; it used blocked search loop with B=256 and two-pass integral image, fast input.\n- Source B had NEON + OpenMP reduction over rows, but slower (~0.0588).\n- Source C had collapse(2) schedule(static), also slow (~0.0604).\n\nThe best performing integration so far was experiment 5578b4f66084451d which combined A (fast input, flattened storage, two-pass integral image, blocking) with B's NEON and OpenMP? Actually that experiment used sources: [\"42835f811f6644e1\", \"5060afd492ee446e\", \"8e2ef19488ab48a4\"] but 5060afd... is not in our integration set. Wait, the current integration_source_ids are only A,B,C. So we must use exactly those three.\n\nWe need to combine:\n- Fast integer input (from A)\n- Flattened contiguous storage and two-pass integral image construction (A)\n- Blocked search loop with B=256 (A) - but note that blocking may conflict with NEON vectorization? Actually in source A, the blocked loop was used without parallelism. In source B, they used OpenMP over rows with NEON inside each row. We can combine: use flattened storage and two-pass integral image from A, then parallelize outer loop over i (rows) using OpenMP reduction(max), and within each row process columns in blocks of 2 for NEON as in B. But we must also include schedule(static) from C? The collapse(2) from C might not be beneficial because the inner loop is vectorized with NEON; collapse could interfere. Better to use #pragma omp parallel for reduction(max:mxSum) on the outer i loop, and inside manually handle j in steps of 2 using NEON.\n\nWe also need to ensure type compatibility: source A used long long for prefix sums, but NEON intrinsics expect int64_t (which is typically long). On ARM64, long long is 64-bit, but vld1q_s64 expects const int64_t*. In C++, int64_t is usually typedef'd as long. However, on some systems it might be long long. To avoid conversion errors, we should use std::int64_t from <cstdint> for the prefix sum array and mxSum.\n\nAlso need to include headers: <cstdint>, <climits>, <arm_neon.h>, <omp.h>.\n\nWe must keep correctness: output as integer (32-bit signed). The problem says result fits 32-bit, but intermediate sums may exceed. Use int64_t for prefix sum and mxSum.\n\nPlan:\n- Use read_int from A.\n- Flattened matrix storage vector<int> mat(n*n).\n- Two-pass integral image: first horizontal pass to compute row cumulative sums into pref (size (n+1)*(n+1) of int64_t). Then vertical accumulation. This is as in source A but using int64_t.\n- Use OpenMP parallel for reduction(max:mxSum) on i loop from 0 to n-s inclusive.\n- Inside each i, compute pointers row_i = &pref[i*stride], row_is = &pref[(i+s)*stride].\n- Process j in steps of 2 using NEON vld1q_s64 and compute sum for two columns at once. Then handle remainder with scalar loop.\n- Use schedule(static) from C? We can add it to the pragma: #pragma omp parallel for reduction(max:mxSum) schedule(static). That should be fine.\n\nWe must ensure that mxSum is initialized to LLONG_MIN (or INT64_MIN). Include <climits> or use std::numeric_limits<int64_t>::min() but simpler: -9223372036854775807LL-1. But we can include <cstdint> and use INT64_MIN from <cstdint>? Actually C++ standard doesn't define INT64_MIN in <cstdint>, it's in <climits>. We'll use LLONG_MIN.\n\nWe also need to ensure that the NEON loads are aligned? Not required, but we can assume natural alignment. The pref array is vector of int64_t, so it should be 8-byte aligned; vld1q_s64 requires only 16-byte alignment for best performance but works with unaligned.\n\nWe must avoid build errors: use proper types. In source A they used long long and had no NEON. Source B used std::vector<int64_t> ps (with int64_t). We'll adopt that.\n\nAlso, we need to include <algorithm>? Not needed if we don't use std::max; but we can keep it for safety.\n\nWe must not change tests or other files. Only src/solution.cpp.\n\nNow write the code:\n\n```cpp\n#include <iostream>\n#include <vector>\n#include <cstdio>\n#include <cstdint>\n#include <climits>\n#include <omp.h>\n#include <arm_neon.h>\n\nstatic inline int read_int() {\n    int x = 0;\n    char c = getchar_unlocked();\n    while (c <= ' ') c = getchar_unlocked();\n    bool neg = false;\n    if (c == '-') { neg = true; c = getchar_unlocked(); }\n    while (c >= '0' && c <= '9') {\n        x = x * 10 + (c - '0');\n        c = getchar_unlocked();\n    }\n    return neg ? -x : x;\n}\n\nint main() {\n    int n = read_int();\n    int s = read_int();\n\n    const int stride = n + 1;\n    std::vector<int> mat(n * n);\n    for (int i = 0; i < n; ++i) {\n        int base = i * n;\n        for (int j = 0; j < n; ++j) {\n            mat[base + j] = read_int();\n        }\n    }\n\n    std::vector<int64_t> pref(static_cast<std::size_t>(stride) * static_cast<std::size_t>(stride), 0);\n\n    // Horizontal pass: cumulative sums per row\n    for (int i = 0; i < n; ++i) {\n        int64_t row_sum = 0;\n        int base_i = (i + 1) * stride;\n        int mat_base = i * n;\n        for (int j = 0; j < n; ++j) {\n            row_sum += mat[mat_base + j];\n            pref[base_i + (j + 1)] = row_sum;\n        }\n    }\n\n    // Vertical pass: accumulate columns to complete integral image\n    for (int i = 0; i < n; ++i) {\n        const int64_t *prev_row = &pref[i * stride];\n        int64_t *cur_row = &pref[(i + 1) * stride];\n        for (int j = 0; j < n; ++j) {\n            cur_row[j + 1] += prev_row[j + 1];\n        }\n    }\n\n    int64_t mxSum = LLONG_MIN;\n\n#pragma omp parallel for reduction(max:mxSum) schedule(static)\n    for (int i = 0; i <= n - s; ++i) {\n        const int base_i_ps = i * stride;\n        const int base_is_ps = (i + s) * stride;\n\n        // Process two columns at once using NEON\n        int j = 0;\n        for (; j + 1 <= n - s; j += 2) {\n            int64x2_t v_a = vld1q_s64(&pref[base_is_ps + j + s]);\n            int64x2_t v_b = vld1q_s64(&pref[base_i_ps + j + s]);\n            int64x2_t v_c = vld1q_s64(&pref[base_is_ps + j]);\n            int64x2_t v_d = vld1q_s64(&pref[base_i_ps + j]);\n\n            int64x2_t v_sum = vsubq_s64(v_a, v_b);\n            v_sum = vsubq_s64(v_sum, v_c);\n            v_sum = vaddq_s64(v_sum, v_d);\n\n            const int64_t sum0 = vgetq_lane_s64(v_sum, 0);\n            const int64_t sum1 = vgetq_lane_s64(v_sum, 1);\n            if (sum0 > mxSum) mxSum = sum0;\n            if (sum1 > mxSum) mxSum = sum1;\n        }\n\n        // Handle remaining column if odd number of columns\n        for (; j <= n - s; ++j) {\n            const int64_t sub_sum = pref[base_is_ps + j + s]\n                                  - pref[base_i_ps + j + s]\n                                  - pref[base_is_ps + j]\n                                  + pref[base_i_ps + j];\n            if (sub_sum > mxSum) mxSum = sub_sum;\n        }\n    }\n\n    printf(\"%d\\n\", static_cast<int>(mxSum));\n    return 0;\n}\n```\n\nWe must ensure that the code compiles with -O3 and OpenMP. The NEON intrinsics require appropriate flags, but we assume they are set.\n\nNow produce JSON output: hypothesis, transformations list (describing what we did), sources_kept (all three IDs), files array with path \"src/solution.cpp\" and content as above, notes explaining integration choices.\n\nTransformations:\n- Fast integer input using getchar_unlocked\n- Flattened contiguous matrix storage\n- Two-pass integral image construction (horizontal then vertical)\n- OpenMP parallel for reduction(max) over rows with schedule(static)\n- 